<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="67922" href="schedule/2022/fall/PS/199/67922.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2022" href="schedule/2022.xml">2022</calendarYear><term id="120228" href="schedule/2022/fall.xml">Fall 2022</term><subject id="PS" href="schedule/2022/fall/PS.xml">Political Science</subject><course id="199" href="schedule/2022/fall/PS/199.xml">Undergraduate Open Seminar</course></parents><sectionNumber>JM1</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Election 2022</sectionTitle><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>In this course, the primary focus will be on the 2022 U.S. House and Senate elections. The course will exam three interrelated matters: 1) general patterns regarding voting in congressional elections (e.g., who tends to turn out to vote, what factors influence the vote choice, which party typically gains or loses seats), 2) the specific contours of the 2022 election cycle, and 3) the implications of the 2022 elections for policy and for the 2024 elections.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2022-08-22Z</startDate><endDate>2022-12-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>12:30 PM</start><end>01:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>223</roomNumber><buildingName>Gregory Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Mondak" firstName="J">Mondak, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>